# Welcome to TimTallBalls 🔧🎮

I'm TimTallBalls, and this channel is all about fixing things, bringing old technology back to life, modifying hardware, designing and making custom parts, and learning new skills along the way.

My biggest focus is the entire Nintendo Game Boy family, but I work on all kinds of technology, both retro and modern. From diagnosing faulty electronics and repairing circuit boards to restoring old consoles, installing hardware modifications, designing custom CAD models, and 3D printing useful parts, I'll be documenting my projects and sharing what I learn along the way.

I'm not here to pretend to be an expert at everything or act like I know it all. I enjoy working on this stuff, figuring out how things work, solving problems, experimenting with different techniques, and improving my skills. I want to document the actual process, including the troubleshooting, mistakes, unexpected problems, and solutions, rather than only showing the finished product.

Most importantly, I want this channel to be useful. I'd like people to be able to watch my videos, learn something new, diagnose an issue with their own hardware, find the right parts for a project, and potentially save themselves money by fixing something instead of replacing it.

## 🔧 Repairs, Troubleshooting & Diagnostics

A major part of this channel will be showing how I diagnose and repair problems with electronics and other hardware.

I'll be covering everything from simple faults and maintenance to more involved repairs that require careful troubleshooting, soldering, board-level diagnosis, and component replacement. Depending on the project, that could mean tracking down a short circuit, identifying damaged components, repairing corrosion, fixing broken connections, replacing faulty parts, or investigating why a device refuses to power on.

I want to show more than just the repair itself. I'll be sharing the diagnostic process, what I look for, what I test, why I suspect a particular fault, and how I work towards finding the actual cause of a problem.

You'll see me work through issues rather than blindly replace parts and hope for the best. Sometimes the first idea won't be correct, sometimes a repair will be more complicated than expected, and sometimes I'll need to learn something new to get the job done.

I'll also be making content aimed at helping people understand their own repairs. If you've got a broken device sitting around and don't know where to start, hopefully something on this channel will help you narrow down the problem and figure out what to do next.

## 🎮 Retro Gaming, Console Repairs & Modifications

Nintendo Game Boys are my main specialty and a huge part of what I enjoy working on. I'm interested in the whole Game Boy family, across its many models and revisions, including repairs, restorations, upgrades, custom modifications, and bringing damaged or neglected handhelds back into working condition.

That doesn't mean the rest of the retro gaming world is off limits. I'll also be working on other Nintendo hardware, handheld consoles, controllers, and gaming-related electronics when projects come my way.

This can include opening up devices, inspecting their circuit boards, diagnosing hardware faults, repairing damaged components and connectors, dealing with corrosion, replacing worn-out parts, and installing modifications that improve functionality, usability, or appearance.

I also enjoy the process of restoring older hardware while preserving what makes it special. Sometimes the goal is to get an original device working again; other times it's to build something custom, modernize an older system, or combine original hardware with new parts and modifications.

Whether it's a straightforward repair, an ambitious modification, or an old console that somebody thought was beyond saving, I'll be sharing the process.

## 🛠️ Hardware Mods, Soldering & Electronics Projects

I'll be documenting the electronics work that goes into my projects, from basic soldering and wiring to more advanced board-level repairs and modifications.

Expect experiments with different components, replacement parts, displays, buttons, connectors, power systems, and other hardware. I'll also be exploring repair tools, diagnostic equipment, soldering techniques, and the methods I use to work on increasingly complicated projects.

Whenever possible, I'll explain what I'm doing and why, so the footage is useful to people who want to understand the process rather than simply watch someone work.

I'm always looking for better techniques, more reliable parts, and ways to improve the quality of my repairs. If you have experience with something I'm working on and know a better way to approach it, I'm genuinely interested in hearing your advice.

## 🖥️ 3D Printing, Custom CAD Designs & Making Parts

Another big part of what I do is 3D printing and designing custom parts.

I use CAD to design things for my projects, solve practical problems, create replacement parts, and bring ideas to life that might not be available to buy. Some designs might be made specifically for a particular console or repair, while others could be useful accessories, stands, organizers, enclosures, brackets, or completely original creations.

I'll be sharing the printing process, finished parts, design experiments, and the practical side of making things. That includes figuring out dimensions, designing parts to fit real hardware, testing prints, making adjustments, and learning from designs that don't work quite as intended.

I want to explore both functional prints and things that are simply cool to make. If a part can help with a repair, improve a device, make a project easier, or solve a problem that would otherwise require an expensive replacement, that's exactly the kind of thing I enjoy creating.

I'll also share custom designs and printed products that other people might want for their own projects.

## 📹 Tutorials, Guides & Learning Alongside Me

This channel isn't just going to be a collection of finished repairs and project showcases.

I also want to make videos that explain how to approach common problems, what to look for when diagnosing faults, how particular repairs work, and what tools or parts can help get a job done properly.

I'll share the techniques I use, the lessons I learn, the mistakes I make, and the things I'd do differently next time. When I find a useful solution, I want to make it easier for someone else to benefit from it.

Some videos may be detailed repair walkthroughs, while others might focus on a specific fault, a particular component, a useful tool, or a product I've tested. The aim is to make practical information accessible to people who want to learn, experiment, and become more confident working on their own hardware.

I won't pretend every method is perfect or that there's only one way to do things. Technology changes, different devices have different requirements, and there is always more to learn.

## 🔍 Honest Product Recommendations & Tools

I'll also be sharing recommendations for the actual products, components, tools, and materials I use or think are worth considering.

That could include replacement parts, soldering supplies, diagnostic tools, 3D printing materials, modification kits, components, accessories, and other equipment relevant to my projects.

I want these recommendations to be practical. When I recommend something, I want people to have a reason to consider it, whether that's because it works well for a particular repair, offers good value, makes a job easier, or solves a specific problem.

I'll also share what I learn from using different products and parts. Not everything is worth buying, not every replacement part is equally good, and the cheapest option isn't always the best one for a particular job.

Hopefully, my experiences can help you make better purchasing decisions, avoid wasting money on unsuitable parts, and find the equipment you actually need for your own projects.

## 💰 Affordable Repairs, Modifications & Custom Work

Alongside making videos, I'm also building TimTallBalls into a place where people can reach out about repairs, restoration work, hardware modifications, and custom 3D-printed designs.

I want to keep my services affordable and accessible. I'm not interested in charging ridiculous prices just because something is old, uncommon, or difficult to repair. Pricing is negotiable, and I'm happy to discuss a project and work out a reasonable price based on the actual problem, the parts required, and the amount of work involved.

Some repairs are simple; others require hours of troubleshooting or expensive replacement components. I'll take those differences into account, but my goal is always to be fair about the work being done.

If you've got a broken console, a device that needs diagnosing, a modification you've been wanting installed, or an idea for a custom part, feel free to reach out and ask. Even if you're not sure whether something is repairable, it's worth discussing.

**Most repairs will require payment for the work and any necessary parts, but don't be afraid to ask about your particular situation.**

## 🤝 Cool Projects, Mod Installations & Community Collaboration

I also like the idea of working on interesting projects with other people, especially when there's an opportunity to do something cool that I can document and share.

If you've got hardware, a modification kit, or an interesting project that you'd like me to work on, get in touch. Depending on what it is and what's involved, I may be willing to install a modification for free if you cover the postage both ways.

For example, maybe you've got a Game Boy Advance SP with a new shell and an original AGS-101 backlit display that needs installing. Perhaps the console has an existing fault that needs to be diagnosed and repaired first, or you've got another piece of hardware you've been wanting to modify but don't have the tools or experience to do yourself.

Those are the kinds of projects I'm interested in discussing. I can't promise every request will be free, since projects vary considerably in difficulty, parts requirements, and time involved, but **it never hurts to ask**. If it's an interesting project and we can work something out, I'd love to hear about it.

These projects can also give me opportunities to learn new techniques, work with different hardware, and share useful information with everyone watching.

## 💻 Expanding Into Microcontrollers, Programming & New Technologies

While repairs, retro gaming, and 3D printing are my main focus right now, I don't want to limit myself to the things I already know how to do. There are countless other areas of technology I want to explore, and as I learn more, develop new skills, and become more confident, I'll gradually start sharing more content covering those subjects, too.

I'm particularly interested in microcontrollers and small computing platforms, including **Arduino, ESP32, Raspberry Pi Pico, and Raspberry Pi projects**. I want to experiment with electronics, sensors, displays, buttons, wireless communication, automation, custom controllers, and other hardware-based projects that can be built by combining electronics, programming, and 3D printing.

That could mean building a small device from scratch, making a custom tool for my repair bench, creating useful accessories for existing hardware, experimenting with different development boards, or finding ways to make everyday tasks easier through automation.

I'm also interested in learning more about programming, software development, and how software can be used alongside physical hardware to create things that wouldn't be possible through electronics alone.

I'll be exploring both traditional, hands-on coding and the use of AI tools to help accelerate development, troubleshoot errors, understand unfamiliar code, generate ideas, and work through complicated projects. I want to learn how to write and understand code myself while also discovering where AI-assisted development can genuinely save time, improve productivity, and help turn ideas into working projects.

That includes learning how to use different development environments, work with open-source software, experiment with APIs, build small applications and utilities, and potentially develop more ambitious projects as my abilities grow. I'm interested in understanding how the tools work, not simply pressing a button and hoping the result is correct.

I also want to explore how AI coding assistants and other development tools can be used together to create a more efficient workflow, from planning an idea and writing code to testing it, finding bugs, and improving the finished result.

**This content will develop gradually as I gain experience.** I'm not claiming to be a professional programmer, embedded systems engineer, or expert in every technology I experiment with. Part of the point is to document the learning process, share what works, explain what doesn't, and hopefully help other beginners who want to get into these areas themselves.

As I gain more knowledge, I want to take on more complex projects and explore new technologies that interest me. If there's a particular microcontroller, programming language, development tool, or electronics project you think I should investigate, feel free to suggest it.

I want TimTallBalls to grow alongside my skills, with the content expanding naturally into new areas as I discover more things I enjoy building, repairing, and creating.

## 🌐 Exploring Other Tech, Software & DIY Projects

Beyond my main interests, I'm always open to experimenting with other technology and learning things that could be useful in future projects.

That might include PC hardware, computer repairs, operating systems, open-source software, networking, home automation, small servers, useful software tools, hardware accessories, DIY electronics, or other projects that catch my attention.

I'm interested in the entire process of taking an idea and figuring out how to make it work, whether that involves physical components, custom-designed parts, software, or a combination of all three.

Sometimes a project might start with a problem I want to solve. Other times, I'll discover an interesting piece of hardware, software, or an open-source project and want to see what I can do with it.

I don't want to promise a rigid upload schedule or pretend every topic will appear immediately. I'll introduce new subjects as I have the time, equipment, experience, and opportunity to explore them properly. Some interests will develop into ongoing projects, while others might only result in a few videos.

The goal is to keep learning, keep experimenting, and gradually broaden the range of things I can do and share with others.

## 💬 Got Advice, Tips, Tricks or Suggestions?

One of the biggest reasons I'm sharing my work is that I want to keep improving.

If you know a better way to perform a repair, have experience with a particular component, know of a useful diagnostic technique, or have a recommendation for a tool or product, please leave a comment.

If you notice something I'm doing incorrectly, feel free to explain what you'd do differently. Constructive criticism is welcome, and I'd much rather learn a better method than keep making the same mistake.

I'm especially interested in hearing from people with experience in areas I'm still learning about, whether that's advanced electronics repair, microcontroller programming, CAD, 3D printing, software development, or anything else I start exploring.

You don't have to be an expert to participate, either. If you've figured out a solution to a problem I've encountered, found a useful resource, or have an idea for something I should try, I'd love to hear it.

I'm hoping to build a community where people can share knowledge, learn from each other's experiences, ask questions, suggest projects, and help keep technology working for longer.

## 🚀 What TimTallBalls Is All About

At the end of the day, I'm here because I enjoy repairing things, making things, experimenting with technology, and learning new skills.

This channel is a place to document that process, share useful information, showcase my projects, and hopefully help other people along the way. From old handheld consoles to modern electronics, from diagnosing a dead motherboard to designing and printing a completely custom part, there's plenty I want to explore.

I'm also using this channel to showcase the work I do and hopefully connect with people who might be interested in my repairs, modifications, or custom designs. I'm not trying to turn every video into a sales pitch. I want the content to be useful and enjoyable in its own right, while giving people a place to see what I can do if they ever need something worked on.

As I learn more, I also want to expand into microcontrollers, coding, AI-assisted development, DIY electronics, and other technologies that interest me. The channel will grow naturally as I gain experience, take on new challenges, and discover more things worth sharing.

If you're into electronics, retro gaming, console repairs, hardware modifications, soldering, CAD, 3D printing, programming, microcontrollers, AI tools, or just watching someone take things apart and figure out how to make them work again, you're in the right place.

Subscribe if you'd like to follow my projects, learn something new, or see what I end up working on next.

Have a repair question? Leave a comment.

Have a cool project idea? Let me know.

Need something repaired or modded? Reach out and ask.

Got a tip that could help me improve? **I genuinely want to hear it.**

Interested in a topic I'm learning about? Follow along as I figure it out.

Thanks for checking out **TimTallBalls**. Let's fix some stuff, make some cool things, learn new skills, and see what we can build along the way! 🔧🎮

## 💬 Comments, Video Requests & Helping You Solve Problems

One of the things I really want to focus on with TimTallBalls is actually interacting with the people watching my videos and helping wherever I can. I don't want this channel to be a place where I just upload a video, disappear, and leave everyone to figure the rest out themselves. I want it to be a place where people can ask questions, suggest ideas, request tutorials, share their own experiences, and hopefully get some genuine help with whatever they're working on.

**I'll always try my best to answer every comment I can.** Whether you're asking a question about something I did in a video, trying to identify a faulty component, looking for advice on a repair, wondering which parts to buy, or simply wanting to discuss a project, feel free to leave a comment. I might not always know the answer immediately, and I can't promise I'll be able to solve every problem, but I'll do my best to respond when I can and help point you in the right direction.

If you've got a broken console, a faulty circuit board, a 3D printing problem, a question about soldering, or an issue with a particular piece of hardware, explain what you're dealing with. Include whatever information you have, such as the device model, what happened before the problem started, what you've already tried, and any symptoms you've noticed. Photos can be especially helpful when dealing with electronics and board-level repairs.

Even if I can't immediately give you a definite answer, your question might be something worth investigating further. Sometimes working through a problem together can help both of us learn something new, and a question that seems specific to one person might end up helping dozens of other people who encounter the same issue.

### 🎥 Want a Video About Something Specific? Just Ask!

**If there's a particular subject you want me to make a video about, leave a comment and let me know!**

I want the content on this channel to be useful to the people watching it, not just based on whatever I happen to feel like working on that day. If there's a repair you've been struggling with, a modification you'd like to see demonstrated, a particular component you want explained, a tool you're unsure how to use, or a topic you've struggled to find good information about, tell me what you'd like to see.

It doesn't matter whether it's related to Game Boys, other consoles, electronics repair, motherboard troubleshooting, soldering, component identification, 3D printing, CAD design, Arduino, ESP32, Raspberry Pi, programming, AI development tools, or something else entirely. If it's something I'm interested in, something I can learn about, or something I think I can help explain, I'll consider making a video about it.

I'd rather make a video that answers a real question someone has than assume I already know exactly what everyone wants to watch.

I'll try my best to get requested videos made and uploaded as soon as I reasonably can. Some subjects will be much easier to cover than others, and more complicated projects may take time to research, acquire the necessary parts, set up the equipment, or work through the problem properly. I won't promise an immediate upload for every request, but I'll do my best to work through suggestions as time and resources allow.

If multiple people request the same topic, that's even more reason for me to consider covering it. If there's enough interest in a particular repair, modification, or technical subject, I'd love to put together something that more people can benefit from.

### 🔬 What If I Can't Physically Demonstrate the Repair?

Not every repair or technical question is something I'll have the hardware, components, equipment, or circumstances available to demonstrate physically. I might not own the exact device you're working on, have a particular motherboard revision on hand, or be able to recreate the exact fault you're experiencing.

**That doesn't automatically mean I won't make a video about it.**

If I can't demonstrate something directly, I'll still try to make a useful video explaining the process, what needs to be done, and how you can approach the problem yourself.

Depending on the subject, that could involve researching the device, examining available boardviews and schematics, studying motherboard layouts, identifying relevant components, and putting together a visual explanation of the repair procedure.

For example, if someone requests help locating a particular component on a motherboard I don't physically have, I might use an available boardview, schematic, or clear motherboard photograph to explain where the component is located, what surrounding components or connections are relevant, and which areas need attention.

I can use annotated images with clearly marked locations, arrows, labels, and other visual references to help explain what I'm talking about. If a boardview is available, I can use it to illustrate the relevant component locations or connections. If not, I may use a suitable photograph or another reference image to help show the area being discussed.

The goal is to make the explanation as visual and understandable as possible, rather than expecting you to listen to me describe a tiny component somewhere on a circuit board and somehow figure out what I mean.

Where appropriate, I'll explain what the component does, why it might be relevant to the fault, what to inspect, and what diagnostic steps could help establish whether it's actually responsible for the problem. I'll also try to distinguish between information I've been able to verify and things that are still uncertain, because the last thing I want is to send someone in the wrong direction by presenting a guess as a confirmed repair.

Obviously, an illustrated explanation isn't always a substitute for having the actual device in front of you, and different board revisions can have different layouts or component arrangements. I'll do my best to make those limitations clear and use the most relevant references I can find.

Even if I can't physically pick up a soldering iron and show you every step, I can still work towards producing a video that gives you a better understanding of the problem and a practical starting point for solving it.

### 🧠 Learning Together and Building a Useful Resource

I'm still developing my own skills, and that's a big part of why I want to share my work publicly. There are plenty of things I already enjoy doing, plenty of things I'm learning right now, and plenty of areas of technology I haven't explored yet.

I don't expect to know everything, and I don't think anyone should have to pretend they do. If I come across a problem I don't understand, I'll try to research it, investigate the available information, and learn enough to explain what I've found. If someone else knows a better method or has more experience with the subject, I'm always open to hearing their input.

I'd love for the comments to become a place where people can contribute their own knowledge, suggest improvements, share useful resources, and help others who are working through similar problems. If you've successfully repaired something I cover, have an additional diagnostic step worth mentioning, or know a more reliable way to perform a modification, please share it.

The same goes for corrections. If I've misunderstood something, overlooked an important detail, or used a method that could be improved, let me know respectfully. I'll take that as an opportunity to learn and improve the information I'm sharing.

Over time, I hope the videos will become a useful collection of practical demonstrations, repair walkthroughs, troubleshooting guides, visual explanations, and project documentation that people can return to whenever they need help.

### 📩 Have a Question, Request, or Project Idea?

Don't overthink it. Leave a comment on a relevant video, suggest a topic you'd like covered, or ask about something you're trying to repair or build.

If you want a video explaining a specific fault, tell me the device and the symptoms. If you want a modification tutorial, let me know which modification you're interested in. If there's a component or circuit you want explained, tell me what you'd like to understand. The more detail you provide, the easier it will be for me to work out how best to cover it.

I can't guarantee every suggestion will become a video, and some subjects may be outside my current abilities or require equipment I don't have. But I'll genuinely consider requests, and I'll try to help where I can, whether that means answering a comment, pointing you towards a useful resource, investigating the issue further, or putting together a dedicated video.

**My goal is to make TimTallBalls more than just a place to watch repairs. I want it to be a place where you can ask questions, learn practical skills, discover useful tools and products, and find explanations that help you tackle projects of your own.**

I'll do my best to answer your comments, listen to your suggestions, and create requested videos as soon as I reasonably can. If I can physically demonstrate the process, I'll aim to show you how it's done. If I can't, I'll still try to explain it using the best references, annotated photos, boardviews, schematics, and visual guides available to me.

Thanks for watching, participating, and helping me improve. Every question, suggestion, and useful tip is appreciated, and I hope we can all learn something along the way.

**Have something you want me to cover? Leave a comment. You might be the reason the next video gets made!** 🔧🎮

## 📌 Keeping Mistakes & Documenting What Goes Wrong
  ## 📌 Keeping Mistakes & Documenting What Goes Wrong

I also want to be honest about the fact that I'm still learning, and mistakes are bound to happen. If I make a serious mistake in a video, get something wrong, or a repair doesn't go as planned, I don't intend to simply delete the video and pretend it never happened. I'd rather keep it available as part of the learning process, with clear tags and, where appropriate, a dedicated playlist for videos with major mistakes, incorrect information, failed repairs, or other significant issues that make the footage flawed as a repair guide. I'll do my best to clearly identify these videos so viewers know what they're getting into before following along. I think documenting failures and learning from them is just as valuable as showing successful repairs, provided I'm honest about what went wrong and what shouldn't be repeated. The goal is to show the real process of learning, not pretend everything always goes perfectly. 🔧
